From fb924f23c8e0bc863df963dced4b593cb75fd6f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Javier Tia <javier@peridio.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 13:19:17 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 33/48] dist/http: reuse connections and offload large
 deserialize

The single client daemon is the throughput chokepoint of a full image
build. It opened a fresh TCP+TLS connection on every coordination call
(pool_max_idle_per_host(0) plus a Connection: close header), decoded
multi-megabyte object blobs inline on the same async workers that poll
every other in-flight request, and rebuilt the reqwest client under a
Mutex held across the entire rebuild whenever a server certificate
refreshed. Under make -jN the daemon backed up while build cores sat
idle, which is why sccache-dist lost to plain ccache on the full image
even though it wins on expensive single-object recipes like llvm.

Re-enable keep-alive on the four dist client builders that carry the
scheduler and build-server traffic (a small idle pool plus a 90s idle
timeout) and drop the Connection: close workaround. The shared blocking
client from new_reqwest_blocking_client keeps pooling off, so the
heartbeat and job-state calls still open a connection each. tiny_http
0.12 drains the request body on drop of the request reader, so a
partially-read body on a reused connection cannot desync the next
request framed on it. Deserialize responses above a config-selectable
threshold via spawn_blocking so a large blob no longer stalls the
reactor from polling other compiles, and hot-swap the TLS client through
ArcSwap so a certificate refresh publishes the new client without
holding a lock across the rebuild. The run_job compression level is now
config-selectable with the default unchanged, so a no-compression
setting can be measured on a fast LAN where CPU is scarcer than
bandwidth.

Keep-alive is the one intentional default behavior change; it carries an
A5 revert marker in bincode_req_fut pointing at the tiny_http #151 risk
so a soak-triggered rollback has an exact anchor.

Two new keys in the [dist] section of the client configuration, both
documented in docs/Configuration.md. client_deserialize_offload_threshold
defaults to 1048576 (1 MiB) and accepts a size string like the other
size keys. run_job_compression_level defaults to 1, which is the
previous Compression::fast() behavior; 0 disables compression and 2 to
9 select the zlib level. The commit adds the arc-swap dependency.

The scheduler status request reads its reply through a decoder that
accepts a stock scheduler's three-count payload with an empty server
list, and sends the client token so the scheduler may answer with the
per-server list; an unauthenticated status request gets the counts
alone.

